# Thoughs about the statistics of the population response
The statistics differ depending on whether we average first and then calculate the delays, 
or average the delays of individual runs.

Averaging first decreases the standard deviation of the bins, which moreover scale inversely to the bin size.
Small bins naturally have a large standard deviation.
The standard deviation might lie inside the 'signal' of the transient, hence giving odd results.

Averaging across multiple runs does not change the std of the spike raster, but improves the estimator of the mean,
ie the signal.

- [ ] How does the standard deviation scale with N, but also with the number of seed. Does it change?
	And with the binsize. And the FR (cf. delta FR also).


Update on the okt 7:

The bootrapping approach is good and gives an estimate of the delay distribution.

The delay estimate depends on the length of the simulation. 
The length of the simulation does not display a difference in mean\median delay.
Longer simulations exhibit lower variance in the delay estimation.
